      Figure 3.  PEPS Command directory Window\page Advanced users are encouraged to create custom icons for new command shortcuts/links added to the Command directory; new icon files must be placed in the existing icon directory (\i Links and Support Files)\i0  prior to editing shortcut properties, generally using a copy of an existing shortcut/link as a template.  This will greatly simplify the editing process; otherwise, Windows acts in a presumptive manner which can be quite frustrating.  Note: This is a severe limitation of Windows that cannot easily be overcome. By using the provided directory for all icon files, the path strings within the shortcut can be safely edited changing only the trailing characters in the path string (which is the icon filename) while maintaining the identical leading portion of the path string.  More information on creating custom shortcuts/links is available in Appendix H.\par
\par
1.60\tab Description of individual commands within the Command Window\par
\par
\tab Note: The five rows of the command window shown in Figure 3\par
 \tab are described below in left to right order.\par
\par
1.61\tab Supported Operating Systems\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, commands to launch various operating\par
\tab systems are located in the top row of the Command directory.\par
\par
\tab a) OS/8.  This is generally OS/8 Version 3D (also known as OS/8\par
\tab    Version 3Q).  In specific instances, system programs have been\par
\tab    included from newer OS/8 family releases known to correct bugs\par
\tab    in the original versions.  Certain non-DEC programs are also\par
\tab    provided to perform additional tasks, such as the Kermit-12\par
\tab    utilities written by Charles Lasner.  These programs are\par
\tab    needed to process specific files obtained externally from\par
\tab    other users usually by means of the Internet.\par
\par
\tab    The included configuration of OS/8 boots to the RK8E/RK05,\par
\tab    generally mounted on RK05 unit 0.  By changing the\par
\tab    configuration of mounted drives, it is possible to boot OS/8\par
\tab    on any RK05 drive 0-3.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the host Windows environment passes \tab    files to the SIMH simulator, it is necessary to include the\par
\tab    standard OS/8 PTR: handler to process paper-tape input.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: Due to a quirk of the PTR: handler, the very last\par
 \tab    character of the simulated PTR: character stream (contents of\par
 \tab    the \i PTRIN\i0  file in the \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory) will be\par
 \tab    ignored.  As such, any file obtained from the PTR: handler\par
 \tab    will be truncated.  If the passed file is an ASCII text file, \tab    the lost character will generally be a <LF> character, which\par
 \tab    can be restored by passing the file through OS/8 PIP or\par
\tab    various editing programs such as OS/8 TECO.  Certain programs\par
        which process <CR> characters and ignore <LF> characters will\par
\tab    function correctly when processing these files despite the\par
 \tab    slight defect.  Binary files with generous leader/trailer\par
 \tab    characters will not be affected by the PTR: quirk.\page\tab b) P?S/8.  This is Version 8Z of P?S/8 configured for the\par
 \tab    TC01/TC08 DECtape with up to eight logical drives numbered 0\par
\tab    through 7.  Over the years, various patches have been applied\par
\tab    to P?S/8 Version 8Z to correct certain known bugs.  An\par
\tab    Engineering Change Order (ECO) log file is available to detail\par
\tab    what has been changed since the original release of Version 8Z\par
        on 23-Feb-1988.  Note: Future support of P?S/8 within the PEPS\par
\tab    environment will be upgraded with the release of P?S/8 Version\par
\tab    9A which is currently under development.\par
\par
\tab Note: Both OS/8 and P?S/8 are capable of booting any available \tab operating systems within PEPS directly using simple commands or\par
\tab by running short boot-up programs which are provided in each\par
\tab system.  In the case of the RK8E/RK05 cartridge disk device, this\par
\tab will allow boot-up to any of the four possible drives if a\par
\tab suitable cartridge file is mounted by proper inclusion in the \tab relevant configuration file(s).\par
\par
\tab c) P?S/8 with the line printer disabled.  In earlier releases of\par
        P?S/8, many programs automatically directed output to the\par
        device 66 line printer if it was available and online.  To\par
        prevent this action, it was necessary to disable the line\par
\tab    printer either by powering down or placing the device in an\par
\tab    offline state.\par
\par
\tab    As of the release of P?S/8 Version 8Z, most of these problems\par
        have been eliminated; command-line option switches exist in \par
\tab    the affected programs that force output to the console printer\par
\tab    if desired.  To allow testing of any remaining problems, P?S/8\par
\tab    may be started with the device 66 line printer logically\par
\tab    disabled in the corresponding SIMH configuration file.\par
\tab   \par
\tab    While fully supported, this system variation is of little\par
\tab    interest to most PEPS users; once all known issues are\par
  \tab    resolved, this mode of operation will likely be withdrawn from\par
\tab    future PEPS releases or moved to the Inactive Commands\par
\tab    sub-directory.\par
\par
\tab Note: Future releases of the PEPS package will include additional \tab operating systems such as P?S/8 configured for the TD8E/TU56, \tab RK8E/RK05 or RX8E/RX01.  Additional OS/8 systems can be added \tab that can boot to devices other than the RK8E/RK05 such as the \tab RX8E/RX01 (or support alternate non-system handlers).  As the \tab Command directory window becomes more complex, it may become \tab necessary to include an internal folder for choice of operating\par
\tab system to avoid clutter; advanced users may also require internal\par
\tab folders containing additional file development icons/links (other\par
\tab than a few currently present in the main folder) for similar\par
\tab reasons.  With experience, all users can determine which\par
\tab Windows desktop particulars serve their needs in an efficient\par
\tab manner; consider the provided Command directory (as shown in \tab figure 3) as merely a starting point which can evolve as the\par
\tab user's needs change.\page 1.62\tab Generic file handling and related commands\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all generic file handling and related\par
\tab commands are located in the second row of the Command directory.\par
\par
\tab The PEPS environment includes an internal folder for files \tab currently under development which is referenced as the\par
\i\tab Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  This is in sharp contrast with\par
\tab files stored in a more general collection of external directories \tab organized by user preference; a partial implementation of these \tab directories is provided with each release of the PEPS including \tab certain example files that are accessible with explicit commands.  \tab This file structure should serve as a guide to additional user \tab directory organization and file development.\par
\par
\tab In the course of accessing these files, it will likely become \tab necessary to interact with both the external directories and the \tab\i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory.  Commands in this section are \tab available to work with individual files in the\par
\tab\i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory as well as interact with the\par
\tab external directories using Windows Explorer techniques.\par
\par
\tab a) Edit Current Program.  The current named file is opened in a\par
 \tab    designated editor, in this case the standard Windows Notepad\par
\tab    program.  The Current Program filename is set using the \i Set\par
 \tab    Current Program\i0  command described in c) below.\par
\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves the updated state\par
\tab    of the current named file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as the input to the PTR: device within the\par
\tab    SIMH simulator environment.  This allows any operating system \par
\tab    to be launched with the contents of the current named file\par
\tab    already set up.  However, it is not necessary to close the\par
\tab    editing program; if the file is saved using commands within\par
\tab    the editor, the \i Send Current Program\i0  command described in b)\par
\tab    below can be used to allow rapid editing operations on the\par
\tab    file after the designated operating system has processed the\par
 \tab    file as needed.  This is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8\par
 \tab    programs while also taking advantage of editing the file in\par
 \tab    the host Windows environment.\par
\par
\tab b) Send Current Program.  The contents of the current named file\par
        are copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used as the input to the PTR:\par
        device within the SIMH environment.  The SIMH simulator is\par
 \tab    then launched in a separate window, booting to the designated\par
 \tab    operating system as defined in the corresponding underlying\par
 \tab    batch file associated with this command [\i Send Current\par
 \tab    Program.BAT\i0 ].  The Current Program filename is set using the\par
 \tab    \i Set Current Program\i0  command described in c) below.\page   \tab    Note: As released, P?S/8 with standard LPT: support is the\par
\tab    designated operating system for use with this command.  By\par
\tab    applying a small change to the \i Send Current Program.BAT\i0  file,\par
\tab    any available operating system can be chosen.  Several\par
\tab    operating system choices are already provided within the\par
\tab    batch file; all inactive choices should be preceded with a \i REM\i0\par
\tab    statement to simplify further changes in the future.\par
\par
\tab c) Set Current Program.  The Current Program filename (used in a) \tab    and b) above) is set by invoking this command.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The Current Program filename is permanently stored\par
 \tab    within the PEPS package.  This command is the only method\par
        available to update the filename, which is internally stored\par
        within a key parameter file of the package; this applies even\par
        if the entire PEPS environment is transferred to a different\par
 \tab    Windows system.\par
\par
\tab    As shown in Figure 4 below, the default Current Program file\par
 \tab    name is \i HELLOWORLD.PAL\i0 .  This command allows the filename to\par
 \tab    be changed to any desired combination of alphanumeric\par
\tab    characters in upper or lower case; while embedded space\par
\tab    characters are not allowed, several additional characters\par
        (such as \i [\i0  and \i ]\i0 ) are permitted.\par
