  \par
\tab    Note: The \i Set Current Program\i0  dialogue box as described in c) \tab    above can be filled by applying copy and paste techniques to\par
\tab    this window to aid in minimizing manual entry where\par
\tab    applicable.  Existing filename characters can be used to\par
\tab    (partially) set the Current Program filename without having to\par
\tab    manually type the entire filename.  For example, the existing\par
\tab    file name \i PROGRAM.PAL\i0  can be copied and then modified into\par
\tab    \i PROGRAM1.PAL\i0  or \i PROGRAM2.PAL\i0 , etc.\par
\par
\tab f) Explore Additional Program Files.  A separate Windows Explorer\par
 \tab    window is opened to the \i PDP-8\\Source Files\i0  directory.  It is\par
 \tab    recommended that all files oriented towards PDP-8 projects be\par
 \tab    organized into appropriate sub-folders within this directory.\par
 \tab    Files stored in this area can be interchanged with those of\par
 \tab    the \i Current_Program_Files\i0  directory using Windows Explorer\par
 \tab    techniques.\par
\par
1.63\tab Custom commands for individual file development\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all custom commands for individual\par
\tab file development are located in the third row of the Command \tab directory window.\page\tab Some of the command examples supplied in this section are \tab associated with the OS/8 operating system while others are\par
     associated with the P?S/8 operating system.  While this generally\par
 \tab pertains to which operating system is chosen when sending the\par
     file via the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  mechanism, the corresponding command\par
\tab icons/links are color-coded for consistency.  Black icons are\par
\tab selected for files to be developed using OS/8 while blue icons\par
\tab are selected for files to be developed using P?S/8.\par
\par
\tab Advanced users can derive commands for development of additional\par
\tab files using any desired color scheme (or alternate icon set). \tab Appendix E provides information for advanced users\par
 \tab to aid in creating new batch files and corresponding command\par
\tab shortcut/icon entries.  The provided files described in this\par
\tab section can be used as templates for any custom additions to the\par
\tab user's system.\par
\par
\tab a) Edit COROUTINE.PAL.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\\Generic\par
 \tab    Source Files\\COROUTINE.PAL\i0  is opened in a new window using\par
\tab    Windows Notepad (the designated text editing program).  All\par
\tab    particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit and\par
 \tab    Copy COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files \i0 directory.\par
  \tab    As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current Program\par
\i0  \tab    command, Appendix C contains information regarding conforming\par
\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\tab Note: \i COROUTINE.PAL\i0  is a example of how to implement efficient\par
\tab coroutines using the PDP-8 instruction set.  It is a code \tab fragment taken from the Kermit-12 program written by Charles \tab Lasner as part of the overall Kermit-12 project.\par
\pard\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
 \tab    the \i COROUTINE.PAL\i0  file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
\tab    next SIMH simulator session.  This allows any available\par
\tab    operating system to be launched with the contents of\par
\tab    \i COROUTINE.PAL\i0  already setup for file capture within the\par
\tab    selected PDP-8 operating system.\par
\par
  \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time changes are made.  By merely saving\par
\tab    the file, the \i Send COROUTINE.PAL\i0  command described in b) below\par
\tab    can be used to allow rapidly repeated editing operations on\par
\tab    the file under development after the designated operating\par
\tab    system has processed the file leading to the need for\par
\tab    additional editing, etc.  This is the fastest way to develop\par
\tab    most PDP-8 programs while also taking advantage of editing\par
  \tab    files in the host Windows environment.\page\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab b) Send COROUTINE.PAL.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\tab    \\Generic Source Files\\COROUTINE.PAL\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment. The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system as \tab    defined in the corresponding underlying batch file associated\par
 \tab    with this command [\i Send COROUTINE.PAL.BAT\i0 ].  Note: While OS/8\par
 \tab    is the default operating system for this function, a one line\par
 \tab    edit to the batch file can be made to boot to P?S/8 or another\par
 \tab    available operating system; this option exists in all \par
\tab    analogous batch files to provide free choice of booted\par
\tab    operating system when sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since a) and b) above refer to a file developed within\par
 \tab    the OS/8 environment, the associated icons for these commands\par
 \tab    are black color.\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\par
\tab c) Edit MEMORYSIZE.PAL.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\tab    \\Generic\\Source Files\\MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  is opened in a new window\par
        using Windows Notepad [the designated text editing program].\par
        All particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit\par
 \tab    and Copy MEMORYSIZE.PAL.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files \par
\tab    \i0 directory.  As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current\par
 \tab    Program \i0 command, Appendix C contains information regarding \tab\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\pard\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
 \tab    the \i MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  file; the file is then copied to the\par
\tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
\tab    next SIMH simulator session.  This allows any operating system\par
        to be launched with the contents of the \i MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  file \par
\tab    already set up for file capture within the designated PDP-8\par
\tab    operating system.\page   \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time changes are made.  By merely saving\par
\tab    the file, the \i Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  command described in d)\par
\tab    below can be used to allow rapidly repeated editing operations\par
\tab    on the file after the designated operating system has\par
\tab    processed the file leading to the need for additional editing.\par
\tab    This is the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 programs while\par
\tab    also taking advantage of editing the file in the host Windows\par
\tab    environment.\par
\par
\tab d) Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\Source Files\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Generic Source Files\\MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment. The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system as \tab    defined in the corresponding underlying batch file associated\par
 \tab    with this command [\i Send MEMORYSIZE.PAL.BAT\i0 ].  Note: While OS/8\par
 \tab    is the default operating system for this function, a one line\par
 \tab    edit to the batch file can be made to boot to P?S/8 or other\par
 \tab    operating system; this option exists in all analogous batch\par
\tab    files to provide free choice of booted operating system when\par
 \tab    sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since c) and d) above refer to a file developed within\par
 \tab    the OS/8 environment, the associated icons for these commands\par
 \tab    are black color.\par
\par
\tab    An OS/8 session using \i MEMORYSIZE.PAL\i0  passed as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device is shown in Figure 8.  The various steps taken\par
 \tab    are documented below.  Note: The session window is labeled\par
 \i\tab    Send MEMORY SIZE.PAL\i0  because the corresponding icon label\par
 \tab    contains space characters to improve readability.\par
\par
\tab    i)   The OS/8 date is set to 06-Nov-88.  This date was chosen\par
 \tab         because it allows the day of the week to correspond to\par
\tab         that used in the year 2016.  OS/8 dates are not\par
\tab         maintained across SIMH sessions.\par
\par
\tab\tab    Note: OS/8 only allows dates in the range of 01-Jan-1970\par
\tab\tab    through 31-Dec-2001 with eight-year group anomalies for\par
\tab\tab    all dates past 31-Dec-1977.\par
\par
\tab    ii)  The OS/8 \i PIP.SV\i0  utility is then executed to create the\par
\tab         file \i MEMSIZ.PA\i0  from the paper-tape input stream setup\par
\tab         when the SIMH session was initiated.\par
\par
\tab    iii) The OS/8 \i PAL8.SV\i0  utility is then executed to assemble the\par
\tab         contents of the \i MEMSIZ.PA\i0  file into the \i MEMSIZ.BN\i0  binary\par
\tab         file.\par
\par
\tab    iv)  The OS/8 \i ABSLDR.SV\i0  utility is used to load the \i MEMSIZ.BN\par
\i0  \tab         binary file into memory; by specifying \i /G=200\i0 , the\par
\tab         program is automatically started.\page\tab    v)   The console output produced by program execution follows.\par
\tab\tab    Since the SIMH simulator is configured to provide a 32K\par
\tab\tab    memory environment, there are eight memory fields\par
\tab\tab    available numbered 0 through 7; the highest memory field\par
\tab\tab    is correctly indicated as 7.\par
