\pard  Figure 9.  P?S/8 session using WHATAMI.PAL\par
\par
\tab g) Edit FOCAL Program.  The text file \i\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Current_Program_Files\\PROGRAM.FOC\i0  is opened in a new window\par
        using Windows Notepad [the designated text editing program].\par
        All particulars of this command are defined within the \i Edit\par
 \tab    and Copy FOCAL Program.BAT\i0  file in the \i SIMH Batch Files \par
\tab    \i0 directory.  As explained above with regard to the \i Edit Current\par
 \tab    Program \i0 command, Appendix C contains information regarding \tab\tab    alternative editing programs.\par
\par
\tab    Closing the editing program generally saves changes made to\par
\pard  \tab    the \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file; the file is then copied to the \i PTRIN.TXT\par
\i0\tab    file used for input to the PTR: device within the next SIMH\par
\tab    simulator session.  This allows any operating system to be\par
\tab    launched with the contents of the \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  already set up\par
\tab    for file capture within the designated PDP-8 operating system.\page   \tab    For most editing situations it is not necessary to close the\par
 \tab    editing program each time changes are made.  By merely saving\par
\tab    the file, the \i Send PROGRAM.FOC\i0  command described in h) below\par
 \tab    can be used to allow rapidly repeated editing operations on\par
\tab    the file after the designated operating system has processed\par
\tab    the file leading to the need for additional editing.  This is\par
\tab    the fastest way to develop most PDP-8 [and FOCAL] programs\par
\tab    while also taking advantage of editing the file in the host\par
\tab    Windows environment.\par
\par
\tab h) Send FOCAL Program.  The contents of the \i\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab    \\Current_Program_Files\\PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file are copied to the\par
\pard  \tab    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file used for input to the PTR: device within the\par
 \tab    SIMH environment. The SIMH simulator is then launched in a\par
 \tab    separate window, booting to the designated operating system as \tab    defined in the corresponding underlying batch file associated\par
 \tab    with this command [\i Send FOCAL Program.BAT\i0 ].  Note: While P?S/8\par
 \tab    is the default operating system for this function, a one line\par
 \tab    edit to the batch file can be made to boot to OS/8 or other\par
 \tab    operating system; this option exists in all analogous batch\par
\tab    files to provide free choice of booted operating system when\par
 \tab    sending files via the PTR: mechanism.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Since g) and h) above refer to a file developed within\par
 \tab    the P?S/8 environment, the associated icons for these commands\par
 \tab    are blue color.  However, since these commands are oriented\par
\tab    towards FOCAL programs, a somewhat lighter shade of blue was\par
\tab    chosen.\par
\par
\tab    The default contents of the \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  file is an accurate\par
\tab    copy of the historic DEC \i King of Sumeria\i0  game known as\par
\tab    \i HAMURABI\i0 .  This file is also included in the\i\\PDP-8\\Source\par
\tab    Files\\PQS8 FOCAL - Related Files\i0  directory as \i HAMURABI.FOC\i0 .\par
\par
\tab    A P?S/8 session using \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  passed as the input to\par
\tab    the PTR: device is shown in Figure 10.  The various steps\par
\tab    taken are documented below:\par
\par
\tab    i)   As described above, the paper-tape input stream setup in\par
\tab         a prior SIMH session is the contents of \i PROGRAM.FOC\i0  which\par
\tab         in turn is a copy of \i HAMURABI.FOC\i0 .  As is usually the\par
\tab         case with most short FOCAL programs, the entire program\par
\tab\tab    was converted into a single TFS file which was named\par
\tab\tab    \i HMRABI\i0 .\par
\par
\tab    ii)  The \i HMRABI\i0  file is then passed to P?S/8 \i FOCAL\i0  which is a\par
\tab         binary compatible superset of the original DEC FOCAL,\par
\tab         1969 release.  The contents of passed binary and text\par
\tab         files are used to implement custom binary patches to\par
\tab         FOCAL and pass FOCAL programs respectively.  In this\par
\tab         instance, no binary patches are required.\page\tab         Various command-line switches, both present and\par
\tab         deliberately omitted, control the precise way FOCAL\par
\tab         operates.  In this instance, the Sine and Arctangent\par
\tab         option groups are not loaded to maximize the memory space\par
\tab         allocated to the program, variables and pushdown stack.\par
\par
\tab         The source file contents do not echo on the console\par
\tab\tab    device during the loading process; this operation can be\par
\tab\tab    enabled bY changing the command-line options use\par
\tab\tab    Automatic program startup after displaying the FOCAL\par
\tab\tab    command prompt character is also initiated by the\par
\tab\tab    command-line options selected.\par
\par
\tab    iii) The running program presents various options to the local \tab\tab    town manager ["HAMURABI"] requiring some strategy as how \tab\tab    best to proceed allocating the town's resources.  \tab\tab\tab\tab    Execution is stopped by the use of the Control-C \tab\tab\tab\tab    character to abort the FOCAL program.\par
\par
\tab    iv)  Users very familiar with this HAMURABI game, as\par
\tab         documented in various DEC handbooks, may notice small\par
\tab         changes in the program output as shown in Figure 10.  The\par
\tab         original FOCAL, 1969 supplies a leading \i =\i0  character on\par
\tab         all numerical output; the output format would include an \par
\tab\tab    extra leading space character which tends to make the\par
\tab\tab    numeric values slightly harder to read.\par
\par
\tab         It is the consensus of the PDP-8 FOCAL user community at\par
\tab\tab    large these features are quirks which should be\par
\tab\tab    suppressed; as such, P?S/8 FOCAL removes the affected\par
\tab\tab    characters by default.  Command-line options are\par
\tab\tab    available to restore either or both historic features if\par
\tab\tab    desired.\page\pard\qc\par
