 met at an\par
    institution of higher learning known as the \i Polytechnic Institute\par
    of Brooklyn \i0 or more informally "Brooklyn Poly" (now a division of\par
    \i New York University\i0 ).  Nearly all P?S members are computer\par
    programmers known for exceptional works implemented on various\par
    computer architectures, especially the DEC PDP-8 family.\par
\par
    The P?S is an outgrowth of a pre-existing organization known as\par
    the \i William Claude Fields Memorial Programming Group\i0  (WCFMPG)\par
    (also known as \i Friends of the Boston Massacre)\i0 .  The various works\par
    of the WCFMPG are legendary in the realm of PDP-8 programming; all\par
    original WCFMPG members were responsible for either starting or\par
    creating various software projects for DEC computers, especially\par
    the PDP-8.  All members of the WCFMPG were DEC employees at some\par
    point in their careers; however, much of their work on PDP-8\par
    systems (and other computers made by other companies) predates\par
    their employment at Digital.\par
\par
 2. SIMH stands for "history simulation" (in the sense of the history\par
    of computing) for the purpose of preserving as much as possible\par
    the functionality of computers generally no longer manufactured.\par
    The SIMH project is maintained by a loose affiliation of\par
    programmers, most generally specializing in one or two computer\par
    architectures out of the many systems supported.\par
\par
    Levels of support for specific computers varies; at one point the\par
    SIMH project was very DEC-centric but now includes support for\par
    models from various other manufacturers.\par
