\cf1 I checked and this is the way to express it.  Consider time-varant systems for example.  But it does need the hypen.\line\line A lot of academics like to speak to loop invarants.  Some of what they say is actually valid, although expressed in so stuffy a manner.  The idea is that if you write a program loop, it often depends on the value of some variably being incremented some number of times such as For I = 1 through 10 step 1 \line\line then the body of the loop\line\line then something like end.\line\line I may be confusing language specifics, but the idea is in BASIC and Fortran and PL/I and I've played with all three in my time, and also got slightly confused with ALGOL which is close yet different and can me maddening.  Of course ALGOL freaks can point out that it predated PL/I but what attracted it to me way back then was not only because I learned it first, but it was trying to be the most sensible one to a programmer more interested in why they wrote the programmer as oppose to how, such as to solve engineering problems.  In other words, I hate obfuscation.  C is the obfuscation from hell language and they are all hypocrites as in "well, I use it so you are wrong".  I't just that give credit where credit is due.  Yes, ALGOL showed the way to some of what makes PL/I good, but it still didn't solve everything in the syntax, and Fortran was more primitive but worked well.  So, PL/I was a good attempt to do it all better and clearer, and many of us used fake PL/I pseudo-code to express what some PDP-8 code was trying to do as an intermediary step to writing the actual code.  That is a valid technique that is sometimes taught  for a top-down approach.  First break it into little modules that can be expressed in higher-level language if it is possible, then implement each of them in PDP-8 code that actually does the job.\line\line Thus, even though I learned PL/I first, it really cleared up a few things ALGOL didn't quite have right, etc.  Thus, I am not any form of bigot, just use the right tool for the correct job.  A language that obfuscates what you are trying to do is not helping you unless you are a flake seeking false job security, and guess what, in the case of C that is often specifically the case [they will never figure out how this really works!]  I know I have to write code that is as clear as possible because even I won't remember it later, and in a large program didn't remember some of the stuff written just a few weeks earlier!  hot-shots don't get it, but Ed Yourdon indoctrinated me and that is one of the few things he got right.  And of course, look at all the undocumented code written in see, or the cuteness of ONE single comment to thumb your nose.  the famous one is JSB and the number.  One of the PDP-10 instructions is JSB, forget exactly what it does, clearly one of the JMP class instructions; that machine has many, the PDP-8 only has one.  Not my machine, so I would have to look it up, not the issue.  It also happens to be the initials of Johann Sebastian Bach.  The comment was something like RIP 1539 of whatever and there were NO other comments in the entire program.\line\line I also know of a comment thus /I do not like writing code when someone is looking over my shoulder.\line\line Anyway, back to one of the computer-science cultured pearls of wisdom.  When you write a loop, they refer to that variable as being the loop invariant.  This is because it is only dependent on the nature of the loop itself.  But some things can be dependent on what it is by design, thus the reason you used the loop in the first place!\line\line But the point is there are mistakes people make whereby they define some other variable in terms ONLY of loop-invariant other things.\line\line Such as  For I=1 to 10  J=3  end\line\line There is no point in having the J statement in there because the loop doesn't change it.  OK, right so far, and then that means you can optimize the code as if the J=3 part is outside of the loop, and then the argument is whether before or after, because it is SUPPOSED to not matter, right?  Well, in some cases YOU ARE WRONG TO MONKEY WITH IT!\line\line Here's why.  Somethings expressed in the loop are actually more complex than they appear, and some of them are capable of causing indirect consequences that are not immediately obvious.  As such, the premise is FaLSE.  You have no right to DEFINITELY ALWAYS do that, and the result is that compilers change how the programs work HARMFULLY in the name of running in less time.\line\line So-called "rational" languages are the worst offenders and lead to inefficient and stupid and IMO obfuscated code because they are not using GOTOs, which just makes it worse, but for this discussion the problem is that the consequences are real but not obvious.  If ANYTHING ELSE is in the loop that can actually indirectly cause changes such as calling a subroutine, then all bets are off.  The compiler doesn't know what is in the head of the programmer.\line\line This is also related to the notion I love to taunt them with.\line\line If a loop should/should not run according to WHILE X AND Y or somesuch, if the X part evaluates to 0, then clearly there is no value served evaluating the Y part, right?  NO, you cannot know that!  The Y part could be something just complicated enough that they do need it to be evaluated, etc.\line\line But it gets worse, it might be true the programmer actually wanted it to NOT be executed!\line\line The problem is not the programmer, it is the very imprecise nature of what I call the IRRATIONAL languages.  They just ASSUME the programmer is as flaky as they are and is LIKELY to think like a little brainwashed moron.  But if you use practical examples as I point out, all the alleged elegance goes out the window.  And typically they don't get WHY we don't want to use that so-called elegance at all because of the inherent obfuscation and potential for blunders.  Then you add on optimizers that change everything, and the net resut is there are programs that have to specify which optimization switchs to us and also which ones NOT to use, and of course all source of incompatible dialects depending on which compiler they ripped off theirs from, etc.  I remember for awhile there were people hired to convert source code that worked fine on one machine to another machine because of the "we don't do that here that way" effect!\line\line In any case, that is when I first heard some smug idiot talking about loop invariants :-).\par
