    OS/8 was originally created as a quick-and-dirty hack pulled\par
    off over a single weekend intended as a demo program to be shown\par
    to DEC management the next Monday morning.  While there was every\par
    intention to write a second system with more reasoned design\par
    internals, management foolishly\par
\par
\cf1 OK\cf0\par
\par
 insisted all further changes be\par
    made to the demo version instead of the intended complete rewrite. \par
\par
    As a direct result,  many serious problems became\par
    institutionalized into the design preventing correction in future\par
    work without major rewrites that predictably never happened.\par
\par
\par
\cf1 I think this makes more sense to the reality of what actually happened. [will repaginate once settled, etc.]\cf0\par
\par
\par
\par
    The original demo (and some of the follow-on work) was done by\par
    Richard Lary, who also created most of the R-L Monitor System\par
    (also known later as \i MS/8\i0 ) along with the rest of the members of\par
    the WCFMPG.  The R-L Monitor System was the first PDP-8 operating\par
    system ever used by most of the members of the P?S.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\cf1 You ask about what was around back in the early days.  That depends on exactly to whom what was available.  I may have some of the exact dates a little skewed, but what was around to the ignorant and my notion are often at odds for a variety of reasons:\par
\par
As I have explained, it was quite obvious paper-tape stuff was largely crap and Richard Lary just hacked PAL III to be the R-L assembler as a quick and dirty fix.  The output was directed to a dedicated area that is somewhat quirky with respect to the $ file and that is one area I fixed.\line\line The binary was designated to start at block 22 because they had already done it that way; when you hand-toggle, you cannot change in the middle!  In hindsight, it was a kludge, and there are others, all of which have gotten fixed.  Given how they did what they did, we can give them a pass, etc.\par
\par
In any case, the binary was written in slurp format to block 22 and following.  The output COULD be copied to a TFS file, but only if it didn't kill block 42 onwards.  Then it was 20 octal blocks or less, and as such it could be saved into another file and then that file could be passed to the BIN program that put the slurp loader into memory displacing what did the job of getting BI[n] the program into memory [remember, that process itself was kludgy!] and that meant that now in memory was a TC01/08 bootstrap at 07600 and the slurp loader and the passed file list, but only five files were allowed because they needed the rest of the page for part of the code!  Rememmber, they really didn't know well how to write tight DECtape code, that came later as a result of all of us working together including Richie, me and most importantly the late Jim Roth.\line\line Anyway, back to the modifications.  Simply because you only had 4K and the FIELD setting directive was not supported, there is just so much code you can assemble, essentially 00000-07577 as a practical matter, the most they could support strictly in 4K on a machine without an extended memory controller.  Thus, a LONGER assembly killed more of the $ file which has always been 0040-0057.  So, the most binary that could happen is 0022-0057 and that could NOT be saved, so it had to be assembled to then load.  So, actually, the input reported to the BI\{n\} program [rememner, in R-L program names are only TWO letters!] was the real $ file [thus 40] and then it knew to use 22 instead.  You could not actually save the binary say in to a TFS file and then back into the $ file and then pass to the BIN program, etc.  Not that anyone would actually care, but it is true, etc.\line\line And the switches passed to PAL III became the =xxxx command and were faithful.  But for the input you substitute the unpacked text from the concatenation of all of the source files.  Otherwise it is just paper-tape PAL III.\line\line So, otherwise we just never cared at all about the paper-tape stuff, but to an outside user, they often naively tried and really got nowhere, it is actually quite pathetic.  It's one thing to try to learn how to program, and it is another to get bogged down in the tedium of playing with paper-tapes, etc.\line\line Sometime before this there was the PDP-5 version of the DECtape Library system that uses the older, incompatible and incomplete type 552 controller where they had to kludge the extended memory break field to be the data field, which is absurd because if there is an interrupt, the DF is cleared!  Thus, it could not work in either extended memory or interrupts, etc.  We never had that, but I did read about all of the problems.\line\line But whe I got there, there was a neglected DECtape Library System and we did figure out how it did what little it did, and somebody somewhere is gonna argue that was a "system" which is a joke of course, and the "Josephsone modifications" was something I only read about before by conversation with that well-meaning idiot Chuck Conley, who was one of the fools that rejected R-L because literally it was over their heads, and later interfered with the design of FUCKEM to add BS from the Disk Monitor System, and that was what was happening in late 1970.  But the Disk Monitor System was indeed sold sometime before, because when I got there, there was one ignored copiy, and I played with it and we all laughed at it because it was THAT bad.\line\line Throught the story, there is stories of a few Poly people who are not central to this, such as Ed Yourdon, but there is another one, John C. Greene, jr.  He was the one that went to DEC to design the RP10 controller for those disks for the KA10, but it was a total do-over for newer versions, etc.  He also later worked for Data General and had a unique persective on what happened that fateful weekend with that egomamiac Bell because he was there at the time and also sometime later at DG as well, then he went to work for a bit DG customer, Litton Industries, the ones that sold the Amana Radarange early microwave oven, etc.  So, he was somewhat of a "spy" playing all these companies against each other for what was best for John C Greene, jr.  [He is insufferably selfish and egotistical himself, but we occasionally talk over the phone, he is part of the "group" to this day, but he lives in Tampa and is somewhat "nautical" at times.  He talks a good game, let's just leave it at that, and he wants super credit for the fact that they convinced him and others to lay out $1000 to help the stupid dead Computer history museum, I have explained a little about that, and now destructive it really is on some levels, etc.  And he's the one to quote about "paper tape is no good for you".\line\line Anyway, he is the one who helped circulate the correct story that a skilled operator of the paper-tape stuff armed with a high-speed reader punch can get work done FASTER than using the Disk Monitor System on DECtape.  Yes, it is THAT BAD, but some naive people probably used it and of course it is documented in some of the handbooks, etc.\line\line So, from the standpoint of slight pre-marketed inhouse DMS you can say that could have pre-dated R-L in the sense of more widely known while those in the know avoided it like the plague.\line\line And of course, unknown to us before I came in contact with Kurt Metzger of Cooley Labs, UMich, they were playing with CPS-4K and eventually CPS-8K with all of its problems kludges and lack of reliability.  And some amazing parallels with R-L in terms of similar kludges and outcomes.  And they had a variant on the option switches that was arguably slightly ahead of R-L but not P?S/8 because we all wanted to imitate TOPS-10, but they used switch formation unix style with -A, -2 etc. and not the grouping form while eventually P?S/8 and OS/8 both had it TOPS-10 style.\line\line Regardless, CPS is ONLY for a LINC-8, although they were trying to market one of the Computer Operations drives on a custome positive buss rework of the guts of the LINC-8 needed for CPS to work slavishly and equally unreliably!\line\line That's why Kurt, a hardware guy, started working with me to develop the software of the modifications to do the read/write DECtapes on the LINC-8, etc. before Dave Soerghel worked with me to perfect what it became, etc.\par
\par
I never heard the full story about this, but there was word that BEFORE FUCKEM, the suits wanted Richie to review CPS-8K as a candidate, again proving just how out of touch and incompetent they were.\line\line CPS-4K does understand the notion of the "sacred" nature of 07600-07777 but it had unrealistic expectations because the LINC-8 hardware is just too feeble to get the job done without help.  Thus, it has a vaguely similar notion of execution that requires intermediary loadng of one component for another to then indiectly get into memory the program at the expense that what accomplished it is not a handler leaving the program high and dry, and also ironically the same kludge as R-L expect that they were including a LINCtape handler requirement that each program had to have its own instead of a TC01/08 handler for the same purpose.  Each are equally bad and caused by roughly the same problem.  The difference is of course, P?S/8 eliminates the problem of premature software while nothing can fix CPS-4K, it is always unreliable.\line\line So, CPS-8K is another cheat that ironically in its own way more resmembles P?S/8 or in a twisted analogy, why OS/8 eventually needed 12K:\line\line 1) P?S/8 The entire handler fits in 07600-07777 with a valid set of kernel info to support the programs that run under it.  We can put aside problem cases like the TD8E and worse, what came later for the moment, obfuscates the comparison, primarily between what was done back then and what existed that was offered as somehow what to do.\line\line 2) OS/8 needs 8K, because there is no system loader in field 0, so double the system kernel size and that function is handled in field 1.  Then the rest of 17600-17777 is wasted with arguable crap in part causes by the slavish imitation of the Disk Monitor System that needlessly makes the system inefficient etc., although not to the extent of the Disk Monitor System because of the laughable file system based on 129 word calls to get one block at a time.  But the point is that yes, at some point in an operationg system you need to have the notion of saving all of the USER memory, meaning 00000-07577, and then if you insist some aspect of memory starting at 10000-onward, if 8K is in the picture.  A good system doesn't demand it is always even IN the picture at all.\line\line And then if that doesn't work, also require 12K so you get 27600-27777, but then take back the last 4 so the crappy external batch gets added on badly.\line\line And of course, P?S/8 solves all of the consequential problems by properly managing memory from the top down instead of the bottom up.  That way, you maximize how big the 15o-bit address space is, and OS/8 15-bit addressing applications such as BASIC and Fortran IV runtime are hurt by it, etc.  But FOR THEM, that came a lot later, while it was quite a bit early in the P?S/8 design, and certainly while OS/8 could have been revised.  Remember, the fewer users running the early stuff, the less it matters if you revise the system to make it better.  And worst case, a conversion program to move files from old to new, etc.  Just bad on top of bad on top of bad, etc.  OS/8 is ALWAYS bug-compatible and maintains all weaknesses never correcting them, etc.\line\line But this is a discussion of what the idiot suits were contemplating and Richie did tell themn it was crap, so back to what CPS-8K is as opposed to 4K.\line\line CPS-4K doesn't support the notion of field 1 in any way shape or form. It is possible CPS-4K was a 4K compromised subset of CPS-8K, but unless the people Kirt Metzget knew can resolve that, we may never know [not that it matters all that much].\line\line So, here are the multiple problems of CPS-4K\line\line 1)  Doesn't actually support the notion of binary loading like all of the other systems.  Instead has a kludge of the notion of building a dedicated image of 00000-07577, that being the same as what P?S/8 SAVED, but this is cart before the horse.  The assemblies of source code are going towards the purpose of building up THE IMAGE of memory.  You don't save it at this point because you haven't even loaded it yet!\line\line 2) Execution consists of somehow getting that image into memory.  But doing so is impossible due to the hardware.\line\line What is in their wacky quasi-kernel is this:\line\line 07400-07577 usually contains a one complete page read/write routine.  Even that can support a reliable read/write handler.  This is NOT a system kernel, it is just a kludge in memory.\line\line If the program happens to be even smaller, then this routine loades it into 0000-07377 as needed and also starts the program up.  Again, no way to include extended memory considerations.\line\line If the code includes 07400-07577, then into 07600-07777 is a dedicated routine to get the last block loaded into 07400-07577 after the fact, but that code has NO checksum protection!\line\line Either way, if you have to access files or other data, your program must include its own copy of a read/write routine, and ironically, tey always recommended what was in 07400-07577 originally!!\par
\line They do support the switches, notationally with - and not the duality of / and also the grouped (XYZ) form, which is better than R-L, but only a pretty good subset of P?S/8 in that sense only\par
\par
So, this really does sound like a hail-mary off of CPS-8K/\line\line In CPS-8K, this image nonsense is continued to ONLY support 8K, which oddly is the same limitation as DIAL.  This is what happens when you do not support something akin to OS/8 ABSLDR or SLURP format.  You have to have an image of what might be needed, but for 32K thats the equivalent storage of 16 TFS files!  Thus, they only did 8K ever, it really is not a good idea, and even OS/8 didn't make that mistake, but remember, it was still Richard Lary even on the demo, so of course there was a binary format not an image format coming out of the assembler.  But DIAL assembly patches a dedicated core image that can never be larger than 8K.\line\line All that changes is that because you demand 8K, they put the subroutine to read or write tapes into 17400-17577.  This just means that there are far less cases of the unreliable tape read/write problem and yes, you can move a copy of the read/write program into 07600-07777.  All that really happened was the problem was moved, not solved, and now you also require 8K, which those suits were always contriving into the situation.\line\line No one ever told me about that when I told that first idiot suite George Thissel about P?S/8 needing "only" 4K for the base system.  He had been the hardware sales manager and he said that might jeopardize memory sales.\line\line These idiots didn'r understand.  Point out that if you do more than tiny stuff we recommend 8K-16K or even more!  And for the later 8/A embedded systems, running in 4K is a plus, since some of those machines had LESS than 4K.  They were selling all sorts of tiny rams, mostly rom plus ram, etc.\par
\par
That eventually stuff got written that realistically does need 8K is not the point, that would happen whatever system was the standard.  The point is establish a GOOD set of standards, not a hatched set of bad kludges that would never work properly, etc.\line\line Users are prone to look to the company as offering a good product, that they had no idea what was going on is no surprise.\line\line Perhaps it should have been called CPS-ONLY 8K.\line\line\par
In any case, that pathetic landscape is what was available back then, so management did have one valid point, anything that was better in some way was somewhat of an improvement, but was it really justifiable as beimg that bad, especially in light of the fact that the four others rejected the demo, having participated in brainstorming it as a bad kludge that only LOOKED good?\line\line Not to mention soon after the input from me and pretty close in timing the other people who worked on the early crap on that PDP-12, notable Stan Rabinowitz who eventually was the main maintainer when they were really just trying to dodge bullets and was responsible for the truly ugly date kludge.\line\line As I have to reiterate.  You have to be some special kind of asshole to break up the greatest bunch of PDP-8 programmers who hung around together and discussed how to make PDP-8 stuff as good as possible!  Or as some have said, way to go, wing-nut!\line\line Let me play devil's advocate here:\line\line Let's say the group came up with a different outcome and I NEVER SPENT the work on P?S/8 which deserves to be preserved because it does exist and has such great application among all potential users except more "power users" like me today, etc. as one among many [but far less than average users by headcount].\line\line If P?S/8 itself is out of the picture, this is a better way to go for a JUST-SHELL system:\line\line For jollies we'll call this the never to happen 8K-P?S/8.\line\line The system kernel is now both fields 7600-7777, essentially the same as OS/8, but the I/O is still P?S/8.\line\line The system handler is removed from field 0 and goes in field 1 just like OS/8, which means that the system is marginally easier to implement, just means less time spent making the handlers work, but hardly any movement on which need more memory, etc.\line\line There would be no system HLT and instead there is a RESIDENT IOS4 handler as in ADSS/9 for the PDP-9 and PDP-15.  It's cryptic but adequate and users are refered to an error code table in a document.  Press Control-C to abort and something like space to continue, ignore all else, or perhaps anything else is continue, etc.\line\line You can do that in 8K, just not in 4K.\line\line And the rest is the same as in the SHELL notion.\par
\par
After all, at the time there is no P?S/8 development, nothing to be wasted by abandoning it, and if managers were not in the picture, we get all of these great improvements, some from me and we are all peers in getting done the same thing.  And of course no kludged data problems or directory decrepitude, etc.\line\line I offer this up because I can see it might have happened if those dummies had just backed off and let it happen.  And maybe DEC lasts some more years until the other dummies ruin it anyway for other reasons, etc.\line\line Now of course this will never happen, P/S/8 basic is too valuable to ignore and just toss, which is why the SHELL is being done the way it is.  The compromise such as it is, is quite small.\line\line That the RK8E was 256 words on physical records is not acceptable. The Western Dynex/DSD-240-8 uses similar media, and then just soft-sectors the extra division.  Just trying to find a mid-point is no big deal in terms of reliability, so we wind up with 32 sectors of 128 words on this comparable disk that can be as large as four RK05 packs.  If the software people explain the requirements, it gets done, etc.\line\line And if not, there is my device handler extension notion using a decent amount of memory to make it happen anyway, etc.\line\line The DSD-240-8 is of course a no-hassle device, the handlers for  both systems are easy.  AND, in many ways it is the model for the MDC8 which has, as a design goal to make handler writing a breeze.\line\line In any case, ending the fantasy, the reality is a combined system solves just about every problem, and done this way is open-ended, avoiding all the paint-into-corneres problems, etc.\line\line A minor detail you fogot:  The early thing became PS/8, and then later the marketroids decided they needed an "operating system" instead of a "programming system" so according to the documents you refer to, there is already revisionism setting in.  We wanted to forget when it was even worse!  An honest appraisal would include the eariest work done, and that makes it late 1970, thus the base year.\line\line In fact we got some "special" near-news letters initiated by Richie at first, and on that list we were referred to oddly as "the small computer buffer" !\par
\par
Few saw those little gems, but in theory some might have survived.  in any case, they prove the lie stated in the book.\line\line\line\line\cf0\par
\par
    Part of the reason the deficient demo was created is because the\par
    R-L Monitor System was shown to DEC management personnel, all of\par
    whom rejected it essentially because they had no idea what it was\par
    or what it actually did; the demo was designed to show how a\par
    computer user could approach the PDP-8 in a manner vaguely similar\par
    to the way this was done on other DEC computers available at the\par
    time such as using TOPS10 on the PDP-10 from the point of view of\par
    a single user.  As such, the managers obtained a completely\par
    superficial and flawed understanding of the demo (which for them\par
    was likely the most they could muster considering their minimal\par
    understanding of the other operating systems mentioned above that\par
    were in place at the time).\page     It is noteworthy that all of the other members of the WCFMPG\par
    refused to work on the demo because of its flimsy nature.  Later,\par
    some other members of the P?S were hired by DEC to work on OS/8\par
    along with a few unrelated staffers of varying abilities.  While\par
    this mixed group did eventually develop the demo into something\par
    more functional, the inherent internal limitations cannot be\par
    overcome without a complete rewrite (which had been proposed but\par
    was rejected by management).\par
\par
    Using OS/8 today, whether on real-world hardware or within the\par
    PEPS environment, requires mastering certain unusual skills on the\par
    part of the user such as working in a mythical year environment\par
    sometime prior to 01-Jan-2002.  Unfortunately, due to limitations\par
    within the file system, it is not possible to determine the proper\par
    year of any particular file because there is an inherent anomaly\par
    regarding which group of eight years in the range of 1970-1977,\par
    1978-1985, 1986-1993 or 1994-2001 is the true file origination\par
    date reported by the available utilities in an obfuscated manner.\par
\par
    In specific instances, particular users know the proper year group\par
    for certain files from personal experience, but inevitably, any\par
    collection of files will become commingled with files from one of\par
    the other three possible date groups; worse still, program files\par
    created today must have mythical dates.  New users must keep track\par
    of the real-world date offset on a file by file basis.  Because of\par
    this serious anomaly, coupled with  the advantages offered by\par
    advanced features of the PEPS package, it is strongly recommended\par
    that all users embrace host editing of source files.  Within the\par
    host environment, more descriptive filenames are possible with\par
    accurate file dates as long as the file date can be set after\par
    01-Jan-1980.  [Note: This still causes problems identifying files\par
    known to have been created from 01-Jan-1970 through 31-Dec-1979.]\par
\par
    Certain intrepid users are known to manually insert pragmatic date\par
    modifications in the output files of OS/8 directory listings to\par
    maintain fantasy records that may contain more information than\par
    OS/8 can actually provide.  However, due to Y2K considerations,\par
    this practice has an upper limit of 31-Dec-2069.\par
\par
 5. P?S/8 is a replacement system for the R-L Monitor system.  It \par
    includes thousands of improvements borne of all-new work carried\par
    out over the course of decades.  Despite all of this, the basic\par
    P?S/8 system is quite low overhead (or in some subtle ways\par
    actually lower overhead), when compared to the original R-L\par
    Monitor System.  On many specific hardware configurations, P?S/8\par
    runs palpably faster than OS/8 performing comparable tasks.\par
\par
    Certain specific concepts unique to the R-L Monitor System (and\par
    not translatable to OS/8) have either been faithfully implemented\par
    in P?S/8, or in some cases, actually improved.\page     P?S/8 is still being developed.  While the P?S/8 release included\par
    in the current PEPS package is Version 8Z first released on\par
    23-Feb-1988, various improvements and bug fixes have been applied\par
    to the system image included in the PEPS release.\par
\par
    As soon as P?S/8 Version 9A is completed, it will replace the\par
    present Version 8Z release and be included in future PEPS\par
    packages.\par
\par
    P?S/8 runs on all of the same PDP-8 models that OS/8 (and its\par
    variants) runs on; however, the single P?S/8 release covers all\par
    models, differing only in terms of specific device handlers and\par
    related utilities where applicable.  It is noteworthy that a P?S/8\par
    system written to RX01 diskette media can be booted to all\par
    suitably configured models from the original "straight" PDP-8\par
    through the DECmate II.  By way of comparison, three different\par
    OS/8 variants are required to support the same models due to\par
    insurmountable incompatibilities inherent in OS/8 internal design\par
    (and at least four variants were marketed for this purpose).\par
    Note: No OS/8 variant exists to support bootable RX01 diskettes on\par
    the DECmate II.\par
\par
    A minor patch must be applied to the supplied P?S/8 release for\par
    the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller due to SIMH incompatibility with\par
    the real-world hardware; the P?S/8 keyboard monitor includes\par
    direct commands to rewind and unload DECtapes.  When applied on\par
    the real-world hardware, appropriate use of these commands helps\par
    achieve noticeable performance improvements; unfortunately, using\par
    these commands is known to destabilize the SIMH program.\par
\par
    Documentation is available explaining the minor (reversible)\par
    patching procedure needed if moving the system image to an actual\par
    DECtape for use on real-world systems.  Various programs exist to\par
    move DECtape images between physical DECtapes and the\par
    SIMH-compatible files used in the PEPS environment.  Appendix D\par
    provides links to appropriate utilities for this and related\par
    purposes.\line\line\cf1 In theory, anything is possible.  They didn't foresee anyone having non-handler access to the notion of the smart rewind and unload routines that are timing dependent and then as a result lock the brakes if on a TU55.  Just emulating it at a more macrol level for the most part, so it is not clear if it can be smart enough to know by the pragmatic changes of motion direction with no other purpose and force the need to find the end-zone, etc.  Just not comprehensive enough support.  OS/8 has lesser commands that just don't wait so they are properly supported by just ignoring them.\line\line If you do not want to support the lock trick, you can just start a command and move on.  The rewind will get the hardware to stop the tape in the end-zone.  A simple unload by deselecting is all too easy, but that cannot stop the brakes.  We had that first, but the extra little trick means waiting enough times to force the tape off, selected the entire time, and then just change the direction.  That means the tape is always selected.  The cheap way is to start it going and change the unit bits, the tape is then totally out of control.  this is what led to the earlier PDP-6/10 FLAP command [because that's the sound of what you get.]   But the newer smarter one support the Tu55 by the smart lock, etc.\par
